---
import { ColorPicker, StyleCounter } from 'pre.style-demo-react-ui';
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Interpolation">
  <h1>Interpolation</h1>
  <p>
    There are two ways to set a value at runtime. They suit different values, and the class shown
    under each example shows the difference.
  </p>
  <h2><code>{'${…}'}</code>, for values from a small set</h2>
  <p>
    Each distinct value gets its own generated class and rule, so the class changes with the value.
    Going back to a color reuses its class. It works with <code>cn</code>, but each new value adds a
    rule that's never removed. The server renders the block's default until the runtime takes over
    in the browser.
  </p>
  <ColorPicker client:load />
  <h3>In a client script</h3>
  <p>
    A frontmatter or template block can't use <code>{'${…}'}</code>: that code only runs on the
    server, so nothing could set the value in the browser. A client script can.
  </p>
  <p id="visits"></p>
  <h2><code>style</code>, for values that change constantly</h2>
  <p>
    The block is static and reads a custom property that <code>style</code> sets, so the class never
    changes, no rules are added, and no runtime is needed. The server renders the real value too.
  </p>
  <StyleCounter client:load />
</Layout>

<script>
  import { css } from '@pre.style/core';

  // Astro bundles this script for the browser, so it can use runtime values.
  document.getElementById('visits')!.className = css`
    &::before {
      content: 'Set in a script at ${new Date().getFullYear()}';
    }
  `;
</script>
